[s0l0!-header][h1]The void Pointer[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A [b]void[/b] pointer is used to refer to any address type in memory and has a declaration that looks like:
[b]void *ptr;[/b]

The following program uses the same pointer for three different data types:[code format="c" codeId="1757"]int x = 33;
float y = 12.4;
char c = 'a';
[b]void *ptr;[/b]
  
ptr = &x;
printf("void ptr points to %d\n", *((int *)ptr));
ptr = &y;
printf("void ptr points to %f\n", *((float *)ptr));
ptr = &c;
printf("void ptr points to %c", *((char *)ptr));[/code]
When dereferencing a void pointer, you must first [b]type cast[/b] the pointer to the appropriate data type before dereferencing with *. 
[note]You cannot perform pointer arithmetic with void pointers.[/note]

