[s0l0!-header][h1]Functions Using void Pointers[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Void pointers are often used for function declarations. 
[b]For example:[/b][code format="c" ]void * square (const void *); [/code]
Using a [b]void *[/b] return type allows for any return type. Similarly, parameters that are [b]void *[/b] accept any argument type. If you want to use the data passed in by the parameter without changing it, you declare it [b]const[/b]. 

You can leave out the parameter name to further insulate the declaration from its implementation. Declaring a function this way allows the definition to be customized as needed without having to change the declaration.

[s0l0!-header][b]Consider the following program:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="1754"]#include <stdio.h>

[b]void* square (const void* num);[/b]

int main() {
  int x, sq_int;
  x = 6;
  sq_int = square(&x);
  printf("%d squared is %d\n", x, sq_int);

  return 0;
}

void* square (const void *num) {
  int result;
  result = (*(int *)num) * (*(int *)num);
  return result;
}[/code]
This [b]square [/b]function has been written to multiply [b]ints[/b], which is why the [b]num [/b]void pointer is cast to an [b]int[/b]. If the implementation were to be changed to allow [b]square[/b]() to multiply [b]floats[/b], then only the definition need be changed without having to make changes to the declaration.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

